hine becomes a new machine, and tightens conformity assessment for high-risk machinery.', updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'eu-machinery-regulation-transition';

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyB$# EMC and radio equipment: EU RED overview

Directive 2014/53/EU, the Radio Equipment Directive, covers products that intentionally emit or receive radio waves: Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, cellular, NFC and IoT devices. Three requirements apply to all radio equipment: health and safety protection, electromagnetic compatibility, and efficient use of radio spectrum. Delegated acts add category-specific duties, including cybersecurity requirements in force from 1 August 2025 and the common USB-C charger.

## Key facts

- The RED has applied since 13 June 2016, replacing the earlier R&TTE Directive; it covers radio equipment, meaning products that intentionally emit or receive radio waves for communication or radiodetermination.
- Article 3 sets three essential requirements for all radio equipment: health and safety protection, electromagnetic compatibility, and efficient use of the radio spectrum to avoid harmful interference.
- Article 3(3) lists additional requirements that only become operative when a delegated act names the product categories: so far only points (d), (e) and (f), on network security, personal data safeguards and fraud protection, have been activated.
- Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30 activates those cybersecurity requirements for internet-connected radio equipment, childcare equipment, toys and wearables, applicable from 1 August 2025.
- Directive (EU) 2022/2380 amended the RED to require a common USB-C charging receptacle: applicable from 28 December 2024 for listed device categories, and from 28 April 2026 for laptops.
- Conformity assessment ranges from internal production control to EU-type examination by a notified body, depending on whether harmonised standards are fully applied.

## 1. Scope: what counts as radio equipment

The directive defines radio equipment as an electrical or electronic product that intentionally emits or receives radio waves for the purpose of radio communication or radiodetermination. In practice this covers Bluetooth headsets and speakers, Wi-Fi routers and smart-home devices, cellular phones and modules, NFC payment terminals, and the radio modules inside IoT products. A product that only incidentally emits electromagnetic radiation without intentional radio communication falls under the EMC Directive instead; a product with any intentional radio function falls under the RED, and the EMC Directive's essential requirements are then covered through the RED's own EMC essential requirement.

Certain equipment is excluded or subject to special regimes: radio equipment used exclusively for public security, defence and state security, and amateur radio equipment not placed on the market, sit outside the directive. Marine equipment and aviation equipment have their own sector regimes that interact with the RED. The first scoping question for any wireless product is therefore whether it intentionally transmits or receives radio waves, and the answer decides the entire conformity route.

## 2. The three essential requirements

Article 3(1)(a) requires protection of the health and safety of persons, domestic animals and property, including the safety objectives of the Low Voltage Directive. Article 3(1)(b) requires an adequate level of electromagnetic compatibility as set out in the EMC Directive. Article 3(2) requires that radio equipment be constructed so that it both effectively uses and supports the efficient use of radio spectrum, in order to avoid harmful interference.

These three apply to all radio equipment unconditionally. Harmonised standards cited in the Official Journal give a presumption of conformity for the requirements they cover; where no harmonised standard exists or is fully applied, the manufacturer must demonstrate compliance with the essential requirements directly, typically through a notified body's assessment. Radio performance testing is specialised: use laboratories with the right accreditation and the right frequency-band expertise, because spectrum requirements differ by band, power class and member-state implementation.

## 3. Article 3(3) and the delegated acts: cybersecurity becomes binding

Article 3(3) lists nine further essential requirements, points (a) to (i), but none of them applies to any product until the Commission adopts a delegated act naming the categories concerned. The Commission has activated points (d), (e) and (f) through Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30: radio equipment must not harm the network or misuse network resources, must incorporate safeguards for personal data and privacy, and must support features protecting against fraud, with application to internet-connected radio equipment and to childcare, toy and wearable categories.

These cybersecurity essential requirements apply from 1 August 2025, after a one-year extension by Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2444 to allow standards work to mature. Harmonised standards EN 18031-1, -2 and -3:2024 have been cited in the Official Journal with restrictions, giving a route to presumption of conformity for the products and clauses the Commission accepted. From 1 August 2025, in-scope equipment placed on the market must meet these requirements as a condition of CE marking.

The trajectory beyond that date points to the Cyber Resilience Act, which will absorb the cybersecurity ground currently covered by the RED delegated act when it reaches full application. Until then, the RED cybersecurity requirements are the binding regime for connected radio equipment, and they should be treated as current law, not as a preview.

## 4. The common charger: USB-C by delegated design

Directive (EU) 2022/2380 amended the RED to harmonise charging interfaces for listed categories of radio equipment. Devices must incorporate a USB Type-C receptacle, support USB Power Delivery for faster charging where applicable, and be offered with the option to buy the device without a charger. Packaging and documentation must carry pictograms and information on charging capabilities so consumers can match devices with chargers.

The rules apply from 28 December 2024 for the listed categories, handheld mobile phones, tablets, digital cameras, headphones, headsets, handheld videogame consoles, portable speakers, e-readers, keyboards, mice, portable navigation systems and earbuds, and from 28 April 2026 for laptops. For importers and brands, the charger interface is now a design constraint with a hard date: products in scope must be engineered around USB-C, and packaging artwork must carry the required information.

## 5. Conformity assessment routes

The RED's conformity assessment follows the familiar New Legislative Framework modules. Where the manufacturer applies harmonised standards in full for the applicable essential requirements, internal production control with the manufacturer's own assessment is available. Where harmonised standards are not applied, are only partly applied, or do not exist for a requirement, the route runs through a notified body: EU-type examination followed by conformity to type, or full quality assurance.

In practice, most mainstream wireless products can use internal production control against the cited standards for radio, EMC and safety, but the cybersecurity requirements and novel radio technologies push more products toward notified-body involvement. Choose the route per essential requirement, document the choice, and keep the assessment evidence with the technical file. Notified-body capacity for radio assessment is finite, so plan lead times into product schedules.

## 6. Technical documentation, declaration and labelling

The technical documentation must cover the product's radio characteristics alongside the usual design, manufacturing and test evidence, and it must identify the harmonised standards applied or the solutions adopted for each essential requirement. The EU declaration of conformity references the RED together with every other applicable directive or regulation, such as RoHS, ecodesign and, for machinery with radio functions, the Machinery Regulation.

Labelling must allow traceability: the manufacturer's name and contact address, and the importer's where applicable, plus type, batch or serial identification. Certain categories face additional information duties, such as the charging pictograms and the geographic-use information for equipment subject to restrictions on putting into service in some member states. Registration duties for specific categories, and information obligations toward users on intended use and restrictions, complete the picture.

## 7. Keeping current: a directive that keeps being amended

The RED is one of the most frequently amended product directives. Delegated acts under Article 3(3) can add requirements for new categories; the common charger showed how quickly a delegated design rule can reshape product lines; and the cybersecurity requirements will migrate to the Cyber Resilience Act in due course. Treat RED compliance as a maintained process: subscribe to Commission updates, track the Official Journal citations of harmonised standards, and review the conformity assessment route whenever a new delegated act touches the product category.

| Requirement | Legal basis | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Health, safety, EMC, spectrum | RED Article 3(1)-(2) | Applies to all radio equipment since 13 June 2016 |
| Network security, data protection, fraud prevention | RED Article 3(3)(d)-(f) via Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30 | Applicable from 1 August 2025 |
| Common USB-C charger | RED as amended by Directive (EU) 2022/2380 | From 28 December 2024; laptops from 28 April 2026 |
| Radio performance standards | Harmonised standards cited in the OJ | Check current citations; cybersecurity via EN 18031 series with restrictions |
| Cybersecurity long term | Cyber Resilience Act | RED cybersecurity ground migrates at full application |

## FAQs

### Does a product with only Bluetooth Low Energy fall under the RED?

Yes. Intentional emission or reception of radio waves for communication brings the product into the RED's scope, regardless of the radio technology or power level. Low-power does not mean out of scope.

### We apply harmonised standards in full. Do we still need a notified body?

For the RED's core essential requirements, internal production control is available when harmonised standards are applied in full. A notified body is needed where standards are not fully applied, do not exist for a requirement, or the applicable module requires it.

### Do the cybersecurity requirements apply to our existing models?

They apply to in-scope equipment placed on the market from 1 August 2025. Products already placed before that date are not retroactively caught, but any new placement from the date must comply.

### Is USB-C required for all electronic devices?

The common-charger rules apply to the listed categories of radio equipment in Annex Ia of the amended RED, from 28 December 2024, with laptops from 28 April 2026. Check the category list against the product rather than assuming.

### How do the RED cybersecurity rules relate to the Cyber Resilience Act?

The RED delegated act is the binding cybersecurity regime for in-scope radio equipment now. The Cyber Resilience Act will absorb that ground at its full application, at which point radio equipment's cybersecurity assessment moves to the CRA while the RED continues to govern spectrum use and EMC.

### Where do we check the current harmonised standards?

The Commission's harmonised standards pages and the Official Journal citations, including Implementing Decision (EU) 2025/138 for the EN 18031 series, show what is cited and with what restrictions. Confirm before claiming presumption of conformity.

## Official sources

- [Directive 2014/53/EU on EUR-Lex (RED)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32014L0053)
- [Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30 on EUR-Lex (RED cybersecurity)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022R0030)
- [Directive (EU) 2022/2380 on EUR-Lex (common charger)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32022L2380)
- [European Commission: Radio Equipment Directive](https://commission.europa.eu/)

Last verified: 2026-09-29. Delegated acts and standard citations evolve; confirm the current consolidated text and OJ citations before committing to a conformity route.
$thickbodyB$, sections = '[{"heading": "Key facts", "summary": ""}, {"heading": "1. Scope: what counts as radio equipment", "summary": "The directive defines radio equipment as an electrical or electronic product that intentionally emits or receives radio waves for the purpose of radio communication or radiodetermination."}, {"heading": "2. The three essential requirements", "summary": "Article 3(1)(a) requires protection of the health and safety of persons, domestic animals and property, including the safety objectives of the Low Voltage Directive."}, {"heading": "3. Article 3(3) and the delegated acts: cybersecurity becomes binding", "summary": "Article 3(3) lists nine further essential requirements, points (a) to (i), but none of them applies to any product until the Commission adopts a delegated act naming the categories concerned."}, {"heading": "4. The common charger: USB-C by delegated design", "summary": "Directive (EU) 2022/2380 amended the RED to harmonise charging interfaces for listed categories of radio equipment."}, {"heading": "5. Conformity assessment routes", "summary": "The RED''s conformity assessment follows the familiar New Legislative Framework modules."}, {"heading": "6. Technical documentation, declaration and labelling", "summary": "The technical documentation must cover the product''s radio characteristics alongside the usual design, manufacturing and test evidence, and it must identify the harmonised standards applied or the solutions adopted..."}, {"heading": "7. Keeping current: a directive that keeps being amended", "summary": "The RED is one of the most frequently amended product directives."}, {"heading": "FAQs", "summary": "Yes."}, {"heading": "Official sources", "summary": "Last verified: 2026-09-29."}]'::jsonb, short_answer = 'Directive 2014/53/EU, the Radio Equipment Directive, covers products that intentionally emit or receive radio waves: Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, cellular, NFC and IoT devices. Three requirements apply to all radio equipment: health and safety protection, electromagnetic compatibility, and efficient use of radio spectrum. Delegated acts add category-specific duties, including cybersecurity requirements in force from 1 August 2025 and the common USB-C charger.', updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'eu-radio-equipment-directive-overview';

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyB$# Selling toys in the EU: Toy Safety Directive essentials

The Toy Safety Directive (2009/48/EC) covers toys: products designed or intended for play by children under 14. Toys must meet safety requirements on physical and mechanical properties, flammability, chemicals, electrical properties, hygiene and radioactivity, carry warnings and traceability labelling, and bear the CE marking after conformity assessment. A recast Toy Safety Regulation introducing a product passport is nearing adoption.

## Key facts

- The directive covers products designed or intended, whether exclusively or not, for use in play by children under 14; certain products are explicitly excluded in the annexes.
- Essential safety requirements span physical and mechanical properties, flammability, chemical properties, electrical properties, hygiene and radioactivity, with chemical rules among the strictest in consumer product law.
- Warnings, including age warnings and use limitations, must appear in the languages of each member state of sale and follow prescribed formats.
- Traceability requires the manufacturer's and importer's name and address and batch or serial identification on the toy or packaging.
- Importers must verify the manufacturer's conformity assessment and keep the technical documentation available to authorities.
- The recast Toy Safety Regulation, proposed in 2023 with a provisional political agreement reached in April 2025, will replace the directive, add a digital product passport, and ban additional substances including PFAS and the most dangerous bisphenols.

## 1. Scope: what counts as a toy

The directive applies to products designed or intended, whether exclusively or not, for use in play by children under 14 years of age. The "whether exclusively or not" matters: a product with a play value for children can be a toy even if it has other functions. Certain products are explicitly excluded from the scope, listed in the directive's annexes, and the exclusion list should be checked rather than assumed.

Borderline products are a recurring enforcement theme. Items marketed to children that look like toys but claim a different primary function, such as novelty lighters, decorative objects or functional miniature appliances, are assessed on their play value and presentation, not on the manufacturer's label alone. When in doubt, assess the product as a toy: the cost of an unnecessary toy assessment is far lower than the cost of an enforcement action for a non-compliant toy.

## 2. Essential safety requirements

Toys must satisfy the essential safety requirements in Annex II, which cover general safety principles plus specific requirements on physical and mechanical properties, flammability, chemical properties, electrical properties, hygiene and radioactivity. Physical and mechanical requirements address hazards such as small parts, sharp points and edges, and projectiles. Flammability rules restrict materials that ignite readily. Electrical requirements apply to toys with electrical functions, including battery safety.

The chemical requirements are the most demanding part of the regime. The directive restricts carcinogenic, mutagenic and reprotoxic (CMR) substances, sets migration limits for heavy elements, restricts nitrosamines and nitrosatable substances in toys for young children, and controls allergenic fragrances. Compliance is demonstrated through testing against harmonised standards, principally the EN 71 series, and through the chemical safety assessment the recast will formalise. Chemical testing should be planned per material and colour variant, because a single test report rarely covers a whole range.

## 3. Warnings, instructions and languages

Warnings must be clearly visible, easily legible and understandable, and must appear in the official languages of each member state where the toy is sold. Age warnings follow prescribed wording and formats: toys not intended for children under 36 months carry the specific warning, and small-parts warnings apply where the hazard exists. Warnings on the toy itself are required where the hazard relates to use; packaging warnings alone are not enough where the directive requires the warning on the product.

Instructions for use accompany toys where needed for safe use, in the same language regime. The language requirement is an enforcement staple: authorities routinely check that warnings appear in the national language, and missing or mistranslated warnings are among the most common findings in toy inspections.

## 4. Traceability, labelling and the CE marking

The toy, its packaging or an accompanying document must carry the manufacturer's name, registered trade name or trademark and contact address, and the importer's name and address where the manufacturer is outside the EU. A type, batch, serial or model number, or other element allowing identification of the toy, must enable traceability. The CE marking is affixed after the conformity assessment, visibly, legibly and indelibly on the toy or its packaging.

Importers have explicit verification duties: they must ensure the manufacturer has carried out the conformity assessment, drawn up the technical documentation and affixed the CE marking, and they must keep a copy of the EU declaration of conformity and ensure the technical documentation can be made available to authorities. Distributors must verify the presence of the CE marking, required documents and traceability information before making toys available, and must act on non-compliance.

## 5. Conformity assessment and the technical file

Most toys follow self-assessment: the manufacturer applies the essential safety requirements, tests against the harmonised standards, draws up the technical documentation and issues the EU declaration of conformity. Where harmonised standards do not cover all applicable requirements, or the manufacturer has not applied them, EC type-examination by a notified body is required.

The technical documentation must contain a description of the toy, the design and manufacturing information, the list of harmonised standards applied, the test reports, and the safety assessment. It must be kept for ten years after the last toy of the type is placed on the market and made available to authorities on request. For importers, holding this file, or guaranteed rapid access to it, is the core of the verification duty.

## 6. The recast: Toy Safety Regulation and the digital product passport

The Commission proposed a Toy Safety Regulation in July 2023 to replace the directive, and Parliament and Council reached a provisional political agreement in April 2025. The recast converts the instrument from a directive to a directly applicable regulation, strengthens the chemical rules, and introduces a digital product passport for every toy, replacing the EU declaration of conformity with passport data accessible via a data carrier.

The agreed text expands the substance bans: beyond CMR substances, it bans the intended use of PFAS and the most dangerous bisphenols, adds endocrine disruptors and other child-specific hazard categories, and bans allergenic fragrances in toys for children under 36 months and in toys intended to be placed in the mouth. Manufacturers will have to carry out a documented safety assessment covering chemical, physical, mechanical and electrical hazards, flammability, hygiene and radioactivity, taking children's specific vulnerabilities into account. Online marketplaces receive defined duties aligned with the General Product Safety Regulation.

The regulation will enter into force on the twentieth day after publication in the Official Journal, with application dates and transition periods set in the final text. New entrants should build documentation systems that can accommodate the passport now: product identifiers, conformity data, standards references and complaint channels in structured, digital form.

| Topic | Toy Safety Directive 2009/48/EC | Toy Safety Regulation (recast) |
|---|---|---|
| Legal form | Directive, transposed nationally | Regulation, directly applicable |
| Declaration | EU declaration of conformity | Digital product passport replacing the declaration |
| Chemical bans | CMR substances, listed restrictions | Adds PFAS, dangerous bisphenols, endocrine disruptors, more fragrance bans |
| Safety assessment | Required via essential requirements | Formal documented safety assessment for all hazards |
| Online marketplaces | General duties | Defined duties aligned with the GPSR |
| Status | In force | Provisional agreement April 2025; formal adoption pending |

## FAQs

### Is a product with play value but another main function a toy?

It can be. The directive covers products designed or intended, whether exclusively or not, for play by children under 14. Assess play value and presentation; check the annex exclusions rather than relying on the product's marketing category.

### Which warnings are mandatory?

Age warnings for toys not suitable for under-36-month-olds, small-parts warnings where applicable, and warnings for specific hazards such as projectiles, functional toys and chemical toys, in the prescribed formats and in the languages of each member state of sale.

### How long must the technical documentation be kept?

For ten years after the last toy covered by the documentation is placed on the market. Importers must keep the declaration of conformity and ensure the technical documentation can be produced.

### Do we need a notified body for every toy?

No. Self-assessment against the harmonised standards is the normal route. EC type-examination by a notified body is required where harmonised standards do not cover all applicable requirements or have not been applied.

### What will the digital product passport change?

It replaces the paper declaration of conformity with structured digital data on the toy, accessible via a data carrier, carrying conformity information, standards references and complaint channels. Build product data systems to supply it.

### How does the GPSR relate to toy safety?

The Toy Safety Directive, and later the Toy Safety Regulation, sets the sector-specific requirements and takes precedence for the risks it covers. The GPSR adds horizontal duties such as traceability, accident reporting and marketplace obligations.

## Official sources

