- [European Commission: Toy safety](https://commission.europa.eu/)
- [Regulation (EU) 2023/988 on EUR-Lex (General Product Safety)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R0988)

Last verified: 2026-09-29. The recast regulation's final text and application dates should be confirmed on EUR-Lex and the Commission's toy safety pages before planning the transition.
$thickbodyB$, sections = '[{"heading": "Key facts", "summary": ""}, {"heading": "1. Scope: what counts as a toy", "summary": "The directive applies to products designed or intended, whether exclusively or not, for use in play by children under 14 years of age."}, {"heading": "2. Essential safety requirements", "summary": "Toys must satisfy the essential safety requirements in Annex II, which cover general safety principles plus specific requirements on physical and mechanical properties, flammability, chemical properties, electrical..."}, {"heading": "3. Warnings, instructions and languages", "summary": "Warnings must be clearly visible, easily legible and understandable, and must appear in the official languages of each member state where the toy is sold."}, {"heading": "4. Traceability, labelling and the CE marking", "summary": "The toy, its packaging or an accompanying document must carry the manufacturer''s name, registered trade name or trademark and contact address, and the importer''s name and address where the manufacturer is outside the EU."}, {"heading": "5. Conformity assessment and the technical file", "summary": "Most toys follow self-assessment: the manufacturer applies the essential safety requirements, tests against the harmonised standards, draws up the technical documentation and issues the EU declaration of conformity."}, {"heading": "6. The recast: Toy Safety Regulation and the digital product passport", "summary": "The Commission proposed a Toy Safety Regulation in July 2023 to replace the directive, and Parliament and Council reached a provisional political agreement in April 2025."}, {"heading": "FAQs", "summary": "It can be."}, {"heading": "Official sources", "summary": "Last verified: 2026-09-29."}]'::jsonb, short_answer = 'The Toy Safety Directive (2009/48/EC) covers toys: products designed or intended for play by children under 14. Toys must meet safety requirements on physical and mechanical properties, flammability, chemicals, electrical properties, hygiene and radioactivity, carry warnings and traceability labelling, and bear the CE marking after conformity assessment. A recast Toy Safety Regulation introducing a product passport is nearing adoption.', updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'eu-toy-safety-directive-essentials';